{"cluster_id": "C-0573", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-072", "B003-063", "B010-044", "B028-028", "B029-001", "B029-026", "B029-033", "B029-034", "B030-032", "B032-020", "B033-074", "B043-035", "B050-044"], "canonical_claim": "`ca-fsck` audits ref streams arithmetically using dense IDs `1..T` within `(namespace, writer_epoch)` and exact-key reads starting at `_ckpt.checkpoint`'s successor. Additionally, Replay starts at `folded_seq + 1`, or at sequence one for an empty base; a gap before the maximum observed sequence is an exception that disables destructive actions for that namespace.; Recovery grounding uses `_ckpt.checkpoint_snapshot_id` as the only snapshot base and `_ckpt.committed_through` as the inclusive frontier; it never derives a base, genesis, frontier, or stop condition from stream `LIST` results. Additionally, Stage A treats the ref stream as arithmetic: transaction ids are dense within each `(namespace, writer_epoch)` pair, recovery advances by exact-key reads, and a dead epoch is closed by an `EpochSeal` at the exact id that the dying predecessor's next PUT would have used; object listing is only a hint. Additionally, Recovery uses `_ckpt` plus an arithmetic tail and seal CAS-walk; LIST seeds candidates and supplies `_cleanup` markers but is never a census of durable records. Additionally, The ref-chain model covers per-namespace contiguous ids, in-band `slot-occupy` seals for epoch transitions, `_ckpt`-gated recovery, arithmetic fold intake, a durable frontier hold, and LIST-as-zero-trust-hint behavior through named sabotage and control configurations. Additionally, The v9 regression pair establishes that treating a scan as truth breaks recovery (`CaRefTableSnapshotLogCore_sab_scanistruth` is red) while omitting all records from the listing is harmless under arithmetic intake (`CaRefDeltaIntakeCore_v9_hintomission` is green). Additionally, For identical backend objects, full, empty, partial, and reordered listings must reconstruct the same logical state with zero recovery LIST requests; forged listed snapshots are not observed. Additionally, CAS recovery uses the exact checkpoint frontier through `chooseRecoveryGrounding`; `hint_log_ids` is not used, and neither `CasRefProtocol.cpp` nor `CasRefLedger.cpp` enumerates the ref stream with `Backend::list`. Additionally, Stale-but-honest reads create a true historical cut whose errors cause retention or delay, while a fabricated cut from omission under a seal can authorize unrecoverable destruction. Additionally, Paginated LIST cannot authorize destructive CAS decisions: a contract-compliant store may omit writes concurrent with an enumeration, but exact-key reads and conditional writes are trusted; LIST may only delay work. Additionally, No stream `LIST` result may decide genesis, committed membership, replay stop, `last_epoch_seal`, next transaction id, owner protection, or permission to delete. Additionally, The design rule that generalizes the LIST-trust resolution: any input feeding an irreversible action must be structurally unable to authorize it wrongly, it may only delay it. A stale-but-honest observation is a true cut from the past whose induced errors fall toward retention/latency and are closed forward by ordering and a final re-check at the destructive site; a fabricated cut (omission under a permanent seal) corresponds to no point in time and can authorize destruction. LIST is only used in roles that satisfy this (work bound, witness set, genesis hint); the trusted verbs (exact-key reads, conditional writes) carry everything else.", "issue_ids": ["T5b"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/task-5b-list-audit.md#L48-L48", "docs/superpowers/cas/08-testing-and-soak.md#ca-fsck", "docs/superpowers/cas/2026-07-28-stage-a-RESULTS.md#what-this-decides", "docs/superpowers/cas/2026-07-28-stage-a-retirement-verdicts.md#verdicts", "docs/superpowers/cas/2026-08-02-stage-b-midpoint-audit.md#t5-3-code-obligations-at-head", "docs/superpowers/cas/2026-08-03-list-trust-verdict.md#trusted-verbs", "docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#patterns", "docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-list-lied", "docs/superpowers/models/2026-07-28-v9-phase-RESULTS.md#coverage", "docs/superpowers/models/2026-07-28-v9-phase-RESULTS.md#list-trust-audit", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog.md#task-5b", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-10-cas-ref-snapshot-log-design.md#core-invariants", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-29-cas-stage-b-namespace-life-amendments.md#make-recovery-fully-list-independent"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0574", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B003-069", "B038-040"], "canonical_claim": "The GC safety assertion is `{preview deletes} \u2286 {ca-fsck unreachable}`.; Every positive scenario requires replica-oracle SQL agreement, `clickhouse-disks cas-fsck --detail` with `dangling = 0`, and `clickhouse-disks cas-gc-dryrun` candidates contained in the quiescent `cas-fsck` unreachable set.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/08-testing-and-soak.md#gc-dryrun", "utils/ca-soak/scenarios/README.md#common-hard-assertions"]}
